# Cloudflare Pages headers
#
# COOP/COEP strategy: was `require-corp`, switched to `credentialless`.
#
# Why the change: the previous deployment shipped COEP=require-corp,
# which is the textbook SAB-enabling setting. But `require-corp` is
# STRICTER than the cross-origin assets we use can satisfy:
#
#   - Tailwind CDN (cdn.tailwindcss.com, Vercel) sends CORS headers but
#     NOT `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin`. So under
#     require-corp, the browser refused to apply the Tailwind script's
#     generated styles, and the page fell back to browser-default
#     serif font and unstyled layout. The same happened with Bootstrap
#     Icons CSS — jsDelivr sends CORS but not CORP.
#
# `credentialless` (Chromium 96+, Firefox 90+, Safari 16+) is the
# modern alternative:
#   - Same SAB-unlocking behaviour as `require-corp`.
#   - Cross-origin assets load WITHOUT needing their own CORP header,
#     as long as they're fetched without credentials (cookies/HTTP
#     auth). Tailwind CDN, jsDelivr, R2, S3 — all are no-credentials
#     public CDNs, so they all work.
#   - Only catches credentials-bearing cross-origin requests, which
#     we don't make.
#
# Net: same SAB availability, no broken Tailwind.
#
# For the embed page specifically, `credentialless` also means a host
# page embedding our /embed in an iframe can still benefit from SAB
# even if its own origin is different — as long as the host doesn't
# try to share credentials with us via the iframe (which it has no
# reason to).

/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: credentialless
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), browsing-topics=()

# The /embed endpoint is designed to be iframed from any origin.
# Explicit allow framing — no SAMEORIGIN restriction.
/embed
  X-Frame-Options: ALLOWALL

# Immutable assets — JS + WASM + CSS + fonts are version-pinned. A
# year-long max-age is conservative; the browser skips revalidation
# entirely on revisit.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Web app manifest — small file, cache for an hour.
/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json

# HTML pages can change on every deploy, so revalidate each time.
/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
